  "worked_solution": "The substitution $z={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2z=6.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=2,\\,Q(x)=6\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{2x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2z) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 6 \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}z & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x}z) & =6{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{2x}z & =3{e}^{2x}+C \\\\ z & =3+C{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{2} & =3+C{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{3+C{e}^{-2x}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+C{e}^{-2x}}}{x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $x>0$ and $y>0.$\n\nFrom the initial condition $y(1)=\\sqrt{3}$ we determine $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+C{e}^{-2}}}{1}\\,\u27f9\\,C=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
